    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d298">

                <pb facs="0942" n="899" sameAs="frus:frus1977-80v18/pg899"/>

                <head>298. Telegram From the Department of State to the Embassy in Saudi
                        Arabia<anchor n="1" xml:id="d298-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1980-03-01T01:48:00+00:00">March 1, 1980, 0148Z</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>55179. Subject: US-Saudi Consultations on the
                        YAR. Ref: State 42453.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d298-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>1. Secret-entire text.</p>
                <p>2. In connection with Ambassador Lane’s
                    consultations in Washington, an interagency meeting on Yemen was held February
                        26.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d298-f3"/> At this meeting there was a general
                    consensus on several points:</p>
                <p> —The situation in the YAR is fluid and
                    dangerous. President Salih’s flirtation
                    with the left is risky and the US needs to be
                    alert to the danger of leftist influence in the YAR. We need to keep in very close touch with the Saudis about
                    developments in Yemen. Recognizing the political uncertainty in the YAR, we nevertheless believe it important that we
                    be prepared to support effectively and efficiently the military equipment we
                    have provided as a means of showing, not only <pb facs="0943" n="900"
                        xml:id="pg900"/> President Salih,
                    but other influential Yemeni leaders that there is an alternative to dependence
                    on the Soviets for military support. Saudi involvement is essential.</p>
                <p>4. Action requested: That the Embassy follow through quickly on instructions
                    contained reftel, emphasizing proposal for a
                    planning conference in Riyadh to discuss specifics of a follow-on program and
                    the importance of funding Jordanian replacement MTT’s to provide training and maintenance on army equipment and a
                    sufficient number of Taiwanese personnel to maintain all F–5 aircraft now in Sana.</p>
                <p>5. FYI: In light of recent reports from Sana and
                    Jidda, we will want to keep under review implementation of these steps, but we
                    should raise them immediately with the Saudis in order to be ready to move
                    quickly depending on how the situation develops. End FYI.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#bold">Vance</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d298-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d298-f1-content">
                <p>Source: National Archives, RG 59, Central Foreign Policy File, D800106–0508.
                    Secret; Immediate; Exdis. Drafted by
                        Michael R. Arietti (NEA/ARP); cleared by Twinam, Walter Hayden (PM/SAS),
                    Garrison (DOD), Richard Aherne (T), Sick, and W. Scott Butcher (S/S–O); cleared by Newsom. Sent for information to Sana, Amman, and USLO Riyadh.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d298-f2" n="2" xml:id="d298-f2-content">
                <p>In telegram 42453 to Jidda, February 16, the Department instructed the Embassy
                    that planning should go forward in order for the United States and Saudi Arabia
                    to provide follow-on support to the Yemen Arab Republic if it so desired. The
                    Department instructed West to meet with Sultan, if possible, in order to stress
                    these points and collect information regarding ongoing Saudi-YAR talks. (National Archives, RG 59, Central
                    Foreign Policy File, D800083–0204)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d298-f3" n="3" xml:id="d298-f3-content">
                <p>See <ref target="#d297">Document 297</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
